-- title:
--   Deformation ring points as normalised Drinfeld triples, naturally
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $\ell$ is a prime with $3 \le \ell$, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number, and $A_0$ is a commutative ring.
--
--   **(A) The full-level rigid moduli problem over $A_0$.** Two equivariance hypotheses are imposed: `hℓ` says that for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$, every variable change $C$ and every `LevelPData` $D$ (a quadruple $x_P, y_P, x_Q, y_Q \in T$), if $D$ is a level-$\ell$ structure on $W$ (both points satisfy the affine equation, both $x$-coordinates are roots of $W.\mathrm{pre}\Psi_\ell$, and the two elements $\mathrm{indepElt}$ formed from $(x_P,x_Q)$ and $(x_Q,x_P)$ are units) then $D.\mathrm{variableChange}\,C$ is a level-$\ell$ structure on $C \bullet W$; `hM` says the analogous statement for the predicate [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt W p k h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) (for $p^k = 2$ the two-torsion kernel condition `IsTwoKernel`, otherwise the cyclic $p^k$-kernel generator condition `IsCyclicGenKernel`) and the transformed polynomial $\mathrm{kernelVariableChangeDeg}\,C\,(\mathrm{gamma0PowDeg}\,p\,k)\,h$. Next, $\mathcal G$ is a family `GroupLaws A₀` assigning to each $A_0$-algebra $T$ and each projective Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ with $\Delta_W$ a unit a relative group law on the projective model $\mathrm{projModelStrCR}\,W$; `h𝒢` asserts that each of these group laws admits a points-evaluation (chord–tangent) presentation, and `h𝒢O` that each has an origin-chart section $\chi$ of its identity section with $\chi(x/y) = \chi(z/y) = 0$. Further, $\mathcal T$ is a `LevelTransport A₀ 𝒢 q`, i.e. functorial transport of raw Drinfeld pairs (curve together with two sections $P$, $Q$) along $A_0$-algebra maps and variable changes preserving the Drinfeld level condition, and `h𝒯` is the section-transport property: the transported $P$ and $Q$ compose with the induced morphisms of projective models (for variable-change homomorphisms, respectively coefficient homomorphisms) back to the original $P$ and $Q$. Finally $P_0$ is a fine moduli package `LevelModuliPackageAbs` for the moduli datum attached to `rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯`, the rigid Weierstrass datum whose raw objects over an $A_0$-algebra $T$ are a curve with unit discriminant carrying a tuple of $\Gamma_0(p^{v_p(M')})$-kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$, a level-$\ell$ datum, and a raw Drinfeld pair which is a Drinfeld $q$-basis; thus $P_0$ consists of an $A_0$-algebra $B_0$ and a universal point $P_0.\mathrm{univ}$ over $B_0$ such that every point over any $A_0$-algebra is the image of $P_0.\mathrm{univ}$ under a unique $A_0$-algebra homomorphism.
--
--   **(B) The complete local ring $R$ and its coefficient ring.** $R$ is a Noetherian local $A_0$-algebra, complete for its maximal ideal, with an $A_0$-algebra map $\iota : P_0.B_0 \to R$; $k$ is a field of characteristic $q$ in which the images of $\ell$ and $M'$ are nonzero; $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ is a surjective ring homomorphism with kernel the maximal ideal of $R$. The ring $W_0$ is a complete discrete valuation domain with maximal ideal generated by $q$, with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_0 : W_0 \to k$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal; $R$ is a $W_0$-algebra, $W_0$ an $A_0$-algebra, the scalar tower $A_0 \to W_0 \to R$ commutes, and `hresR₀` says $\mathrm{res}_R \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}_{W_0,R} = \mathrm{res}_0$. The hypothesis `hfac` expresses that $(R, \iota)$ solves the pointed factorisation problem: for every Artinian local ring $T$ which is a $W_0$- and $A_0$-algebra with commuting tower, equipped with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal of $T$ and with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}_{W_0,T} = \mathrm{res}_0$, and every $A_0$-algebra map $\varphi : P_0.B_0 \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$, there is a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : R \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \Phi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and $\Phi \circ \iota = \varphi$.
--
--   **(C) The étale-level package over $W_0$.** The hypotheses `hℓ'` and `hM'` are the variable-change equivariance statements of (A) over $W_0$-algebras, and `h𝒢r`, `h𝒢Or` assert that $\mathcal G.\mathrm{restrictScalars}\,W_0$ is chord–tangent and has the origin-identity property. $P_{\mathrm{et}}$ is a fine moduli package over $W_0$ for the rigid datum built from the $\Gamma_0(M')$-component times the product of the level-$\ell$ component and the trivial component, that is, for the same problem with the Drinfeld $q$-level slot removed; $x_{\mathrm{et}}$ is a raw object of that datum over $P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0$ and `hxet` says its class in the quotient by variable changes is the universal point $P_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{univ}$.
--
--   **(D) The $q$-torsion-representing algebra.** $CQ$ is a commutative ring which is both a $P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0$-algebra and a $W_0$-algebra, compatibly; $Q_u$ is a section of the projective model of $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ over $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the structure map $P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0 \to CQ$; `hQu` says $[q]Q_u$ equals the identity section for the restricted group law at $(P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0, x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}, x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{isUnit}_\Delta)$; and `hrep` says that $CQ$ represents the $q$-torsion: for every $P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0$-algebra $T$ and every $T$-point $Q$ of the curve, $[q]Q$ is the identity section if and only if there is a unique $P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0$-algebra map $\chi : CQ \to T$ such that $\mathrm{Spec}\,\chi$ followed by $Q_u$ is $Q$.
--
--   **(E) The torsion-lift ring $S$ and the formal group.** $S$ is a Noetherian local $W_0$-algebra, complete for its maximal ideal, with a $W_0$-algebra map $\iota_S : CQ \to S$, a surjection $\mathrm{res}_S : S \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal and with $\mathrm{res}_S \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}_{W_0,S} = \mathrm{res}_0$, and `hfacS` is the factorisation property of $(S, \iota_S)$ over Artinian local $W_0$-algebras $T$ with residue map to $k$ over $\mathrm{res}_0$: every $W_0$-algebra map $\varphi : CQ \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S$ extends to a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : S \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \Phi = \mathrm{res}_S$ and $\Phi \circ \iota_S = \varphi$. Moreover $F_S$ is a formal group over $S$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law of $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ pushed forward along $\iota_S \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}_{P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0, CQ}$, and its $q$-series factors as $F_S.\mathrm{nthSeries}\,q = X \cdot g \cdot v$ with $v$ a unit power series and $g \in S[X]$ monic of degree $q-1$ all of whose coefficients in degrees $< q-1$ lie in the maximal ideal of $S$ and whose constant coefficient is $q$ times a unit.
--
--   **(F) The residual point over $k$.** The field $k$ is an $A_0$-algebra, a $W_0$-algebra with commuting tower and a $P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0$-algebra, with $\mathrm{algebraMap}_{W_0,k} = \mathrm{res}_0$. Further data: $\rho : P_0.B_0 \to_{A_0} k$ with $\rho = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$; $\psi_{\mathrm{et}} : P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0 \to_{W_0} k$ with $\psi_{\mathrm{et}}(b) = \mathrm{res}_S(\iota_S(\mathrm{algebraMap}\,b))$ and with $\mathrm{algebraMap}_{P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0,k} = \psi_{\mathrm{et}}$; the hypothesis $\mathrm{h}\Delta_k$ that the discriminant of the reduced curve $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ mapped along $\psi_{\mathrm{et}}$ is a unit; a section $Q_k$ of that reduced curve; the pinning hypothesis `hQk_pin`, which asserts the existence of a graded ring homomorphism $\phi$ from the projective-model grading of $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ to that of its reduction, with the irrelevant-ideal inclusion, which is a coefficient homomorphism for $\psi_{\mathrm{et}}$ and for which $Q_k$ followed by $\mathrm{Proj}.\mathrm{map}\,\phi$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S)$ followed by $Q_u$; the hypothesis `hQk` that $Q_k$ is not the identity section of $\mathcal G$ at the reduced curve; `hhk`, that for each prime $p \mid M'$ the reduced polynomial $(x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{level}.1\,p)$ satisfies `IsGamma0PowAt` for $(p, v_p(M'))$ on the reduced curve; `hDk`, that the reduced level-$\ell$ datum is a level-$\ell$ structure on the reduced curve; `hyk`, that the raw Drinfeld pair consisting of the reduced curve together with the identity section and $Q_k$ satisfies the level condition `RawDrinfeldPair.IsLevel 𝒢 q` (the curve agrees and the pair is a Drinfeld $q$-basis, i.e. its basis divisor ideal equals the $q$-torsion ideal); and finally `hρyk`, that the image of $P_0.\mathrm{univ}$ under $\rho$ is the class of the explicit raw object over $k$ assembled from the reduced curve, $\mathrm{h}\Delta_k$, the reduced $\Gamma_0(M')$-tuple, the reduced level-$\ell$ datum and the pair $(O, Q_k)$, with level proofs $(\mathrm{hhk}, \mathrm{hDk}, \mathrm{hyk})$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses there exists a family $\alpha$ which, for every Artinian local ring $T$ that is a $W_0$-algebra and every surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal of $T$ and satisfying $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}_{W_0,T} = \mathrm{res}_0$, gives a bijection
--   $$(R \to_{W_0} T) \;\simeq\; \{ d = (\psi, (P,Q)) \},$$
--   where $\psi$ ranges over $W_0$-algebra maps $P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0 \to T$ and $P, Q$ over morphisms $\mathrm{Spec}\,T \to \mathrm{projModelCR}\,(x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve})$, subject to the following four conditions:
--
--   1. $\mathrm{res}_T(\psi(b)) = \psi_{\mathrm{et}}(b)$ for all $b \in P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0$;
--
--   2. there are witnesses $h_P$, $h_Q$ that $P$ and $Q$ composed with the structure morphism $\mathrm{projModelStrCR}(x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve})$ both equal $\mathrm{Spec}\,\psi$, such that for the group law $(\mathcal G.\mathrm{restrictScalars}\,W_0)$ at $(P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0, x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}, x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{isUnit}_\Delta)$ the pair $(\langle P, h_P\rangle, \langle Q, h_Q\rangle)$ is a relative Drinfeld $q$-basis over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\psi$ (the relative basis divisor ideal sheaf datum equals the relative $q$-torsion ideal sheaf datum) and moreover $[q]\langle Q, h_Q\rangle$ is the identity section;
--
--   3. $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathrm{res}_T$ followed by $P$ equals the underlying morphism of the identity section of that group law over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\psi_{\mathrm{et}}$;
--
--   4. $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathrm{res}_T$ followed by $Q$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S)$ followed by $Q_u$.
--
--   These bijections are natural in the following sense: for all $T$, $\mathrm{res}_T$ and $T'$, $\mathrm{res}_{T'}$ as above, every $W_0$-algebra map $f : T \to T'$ with $\mathrm{res}_{T'} \circ f = \mathrm{res}_T$, and every $\varphi : R \to_{W_0} T$, the triple underlying $\alpha_{T'}(f \circ \varphi)$ is
--   $$\bigl(f \circ \psi,\; \mathrm{Spec}\,f \text{ followed by } P,\; \mathrm{Spec}\,f \text{ followed by } Q\bigr),$$
--   where $(\psi, (P,Q))$ is the triple underlying $\alpha_T(\varphi)$.
--
--   This is the normal-form description, in the style of Katz–Mazur's analysis of deformations at an ordinary point, of the functor of Artinian points of the complete local ring $R$ of the full-level ($\Gamma_0(M')$, level-$\ell$, Drinfeld $\Gamma(q)$) moduli problem at a residual point: such a point is the same as a point of the étale-level moduli problem together with a relative Drinfeld $q$-basis $(P,Q)$ of the universal curve whose first member reduces to the origin and whose second member reduces to the fixed $q$-torsion point $Q_u$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.exists_algHom_equiv_subtype_eval_map_eq_zero_natural_of_factorsThrough_of_nthSeries_eq_X_mul_mul_of_isDomain_adjoinRoot_gamma0Pow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.exists_algHom_equiv_subtype_eval_map_eq_zero_natural_of_factorsThrough_of_nthSeries_eq_X_mul_mul_of_isDomain_adjoinRoot_gamma0Pow), where the triples are further identified with roots of the degree-$(q-1)$ factor $g$ of the $q$-series of the formal group.
